# ConContexto — environment variable reference
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in the secrets:
#
#     cp env.example .env
#
# Docker Compose reads `.env` from the repository root automatically.
# `.env` is git-ignored; this file is committed and MUST stay in sync with
# what the code actually reads (see "Keeping this file honest" at the end).
#
# Every variable below was traced from source, not assumed:
#   POSTGRES_*      -> docker-compose.yml, scripts/backup/pg_dump.sh
#   DATABASE_URL    -> app/cmd/concontexto/migrate_cmd.go
#   PORT            -> app/cmd/concontexto/serve.go, app/internal/healthcheck
#   STATIC_ROOT     -> app/cmd/concontexto/serve.go
#   POSTGRES_ADDR   -> app/internal/healthcheck
#   APP_DATA_ROOT   -> app/cmd/concontexto/ingest_cmd.go
#   APP_SCHEDULE_INTERVAL -> app/cmd/concontexto/schedule.go
#   GITHUB_DISPATCH_REPO, GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN -> app/cmd/concontexto/ingest_cmd.go
#   APP_PUBLISH_RETAIN_ARTIFACTS -> app/cmd/concontexto/ingest_cmd.go
#   APP_PUBLISH_LATENCY_BUDGET   -> app/cmd/concontexto/schedule.go
#   EXPORT_URL, EXPORT_DIR       -> web/src/lib/export/loader.ts
#   BUILD_WITH_SYNTHETIC_FIXTURE -> web/src/lib/export/loader.ts
#   WORKBENCH                    -> web/astro.config.mjs
#
# NOTE the last three: `scripts/check-env-example.sh` only scans the GO
# sources, so nothing enforced the presence of the WEB build's variables here.
# They were absent from this file, from docs/ and from openspec/config.yaml
# entirely (verify-report CRITICAL-15) while one of them decided which numbers
# the production image published. Documented below, under "WEB BUILD".


# =============================================================================
# REQUIRED — deployment fails without this
# =============================================================================

# PostgreSQL superuser password for the stack's database.
# docker-compose.yml declares this with `:?` so Compose REFUSES to start
# rather than silently booting with an empty password. There is deliberately
# no default. Set it in the Portainer stack environment or in `.env`, never
# in a committed file.
#
# Generate a strong value with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=


# =============================================================================
# DATABASE — defaults match docker-compose.yml
# =============================================================================

# Database name and role. The backup script (scripts/backup/pg_dump.sh)
# defaults to these same values, so overriding one means overriding it for
# backups too.
POSTGRES_DB=concontexto
POSTGRES_USER=concontexto

# Connection string used by the `migrate` subcommand and, from slice 5a
# onwards, by the ingestion adapters.
#
# Inside the Compose stack this is BUILT AUTOMATICALLY from POSTGRES_USER /
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD / POSTGRES_DB — you do NOT need to set it there.
# Set it only when running the binary or the tests from the host, pointed at
# a database you reached some other way.
#
# `sslmode=disable` is acceptable for the in-stack connection ONLY because
# PRD §14.3 puts Postgres on the `internal` Docker network with no published
# ports: it is unreachable from the host and from Nginx Proxy Manager. If
# that ever changes, this must change with it.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://concontexto:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/concontexto?sslmode=disable

# Host:port used by `healthcheck --deep` for a credential-free TCP
# reachability probe. Deliberately separate from DATABASE_URL: the shallow
# probe must stay usable when no credentials are configured.
POSTGRES_ADDR=postgres:5432


# =============================================================================
# APPLICATION RUNTIME — the image already sets sane defaults
# =============================================================================

# HTTP listen port. Baked into the image as ENV PORT=8080; override only to
# run on a different port. Nginx Proxy Manager reaches the container by name
# on this port over the shared `proxy` network — no port is published to the
# host.
PORT=8080

# Directory the static handler serves from. Baked into the image as
# ENV STATIC_ROOT=/web/dist, where the `public_html` volume is mounted.
# The Go default is the RELATIVE path `web/dist`, which is why the image
# sets the absolute one explicitly — do not rely on the working directory.
# Override only when running `serve` from a source checkout.
STATIC_ROOT=/web/dist

# Directory the `ingest` subcommand archives raw downloads and the raw-
# file hash listing under (spec raw-file-archive; PRD §14.2). The Go
# default is the fixed path `/app_data` — docker-compose.yml's own
# `app_data` volume mount — so this normally needs no override inside the
# Compose stack. Override only when running `ingest --series`/`--source`
# from a source checkout, mirroring STATIC_ROOT's own convention.
# APP_DATA_ROOT=/app_data

# How long `serve`'s in-process scheduler (verify-report CRITICAL C4) waits
# after a SUCCESSFUL scheduled cycle for a source before running it again.
# A Go duration string (e.g. "24h", "30m"). The Go default is 24h,
# matching PRD §9.2's publication-cadence language. A FAILED cycle instead
# retries at the scheduler's own backoff, never this interval. An unset
# or unparsable value falls back to the default and is logged, never fatal.
# APP_SCHEDULE_INTERVAL=24h

# Rebuild-trigger dispatcher (design D-2, slice 4): after a publishing
# cycle in which at least one series published, `ingest` POSTs a GitHub
# repository_dispatch to trigger the site rebuild. Both variables must be
# set for dispatch to run; either unset means "not configured" and
# publishing.Publish simply skips dispatch (never fails the ingest).
# Deliberately named apart from the GitHub-Actions-reserved GITHUB_*
# family (e.g. GITHUB_REPOSITORY, auto-set inside every Actions runner) so
# this production-side value can never be shadowed by one Actions itself
# injects.
#
# GITHUB_DISPATCH_REPO=jorgealonsodev/concontexto

# Fine-grained personal access token scoped to Contents: read and
# Actions: write on GITHUB_DISPATCH_REPO only. Generate one at
# https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens — never the classic,
# account-wide token type.
# GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN=

# Artifact retention (task 4.9/4.10, slice 4): after each successful
# publish cycle, the exported artifact is archived into a timestamped
# snapshot under APP_DATA_ROOT/data-derived-history, so a bad publish can
# be rolled back. This sets how many snapshots are kept before the oldest
# are pruned. A Go integer, e.g. "10". Never honoured below 5 (the
# design's own configured floor) -- an unset, unparsable or sub-floor
# value falls back to the 5-artifact default and is logged, never fatal.
# The live, currently-served artifact under STATIC_ROOT/data-derived is
# never itself a candidate for pruning.
# APP_PUBLISH_RETAIN_ARTIFACTS=5

# Publish-latency budget (task 4.11/4.12, slice 4): the scheduler's
# publish-latency watchdog raises an operational alert (never rendered to
# a reader page) when a source's most recent successful ingestion has not
# yet been reflected in the local export after this much time has
# elapsed. A Go duration string (e.g. "30m"). The Go default is 30
# minutes (spec pipeline-operations, "MUST default to 30 minutes"), well
# inside PRD §19.3's under-24-hour freshness target. An unset or
# unparsable value falls back to the default and is logged, never fatal.
# APP_PUBLISH_LATENCY_BUDGET=30m


# =============================================================================
# WEB BUILD — read by the Astro build, never by the Go binary
# =============================================================================
#
# These are BUILD-TIME variables. They are not read by the running container
# and they do not belong in the Portainer stack environment: they belong to
# whatever runs `astro build` — the Dockerfile's web-builder stage (as
# `--build-arg`), a CI job, or a developer's shell.
#
# They exist because the site is fully pre-rendered (PRD §14.2: no Node in
# production, zero queries at request time). The Go pipeline writes ONE JSON
# artifact (`concontexto export` -> STATIC_ROOT/data-derived) and the Astro
# build reads it, digest-verifies it and renders it. These variables say WHERE
# the build reads it from — which is the same as saying WHICH NUMBERS THE SITE
# PUBLISHES.
#
# EXACTLY ONE of the three must be set. There is no default and no fallback:
# `resolveLoadOptionsFromEnv` (web/src/lib/export/loader.ts) throws, and the
# build exits non-zero, when none is. That refusal is verify-report
# CRITICAL-15's fix — see the "Why there is no default" note at the end of this
# block.

# The live, already-published /data-derived/ origin, fetched over HTTP at build
# time (design.md D-1's "EXPORT_URL live" half). This is the production shape:
# a publishing cycle writes the artifact into the container's public_html
# volume, POSTs a repository_dispatch (GITHUB_DISPATCH_* above), and the
# rebuild fetches the artifact from the site it is rebuilding. Trailing slash
# optional. Configured for the real deploy as the `EXPORT_URL` repository
# VARIABLE, not a secret — it is a public URL (.github/workflows/deploy.yml).
#
# EXPORT_URL=https://concontexto.example/data-derived/

# A directory holding manifest.json + series/*.json, read off the filesystem
# (design.md D-1's "EXPORT_DIR fixture" half). Absolute, or relative to the
# `astro build` working directory (`web/`).
#
# Used by:
#   - .github/workflows/ingest-export-build.yml, pointed at the artifact its Go
#     end-to-end export just wrote — the one CI chain that builds against REAL
#     data;
#   - the container build, when there is no live origin yet to fetch from (the
#     first deploy): stage the artifact under `web/` and pass
#     `--build-arg EXPORT_DIR=data-derived`. See the Dockerfile's web-builder
#     stage for the full invocation.
#
# EXPORT_DIR=/path/to/data-derived

# Deliberate opt-in to the SYNTHETIC golden fixture at
# `web/test/fixtures/export`. Must be exactly "1" (the same convention
# WORKBENCH uses below); any other value, including "true", is refused.
#
# READ web/test/fixtures/export/source.txt BEFORE SETTING THIS. That fixture is
# 98 to 294 observations per series of which only the newest THREE are real:
# everything before them is a straight line produced by a formula, carrying
# real INE attribution, real origin identifiers and a real extraction
# timestamp. It exists so tests and local pages have a full-length series to
# render. Anything built with this variable set MUST NOT be published.
#
# Set by: `npm run dev`, `npm run build:fixture`, `playwright.config.ts`'s
# webServer command, and ci.yml's production-shape build. NEVER by the
# Dockerfile — `.dockerignore` removes the fixture from the build context, so
# there is nothing for it to unlock there.
#
# BUILD_WITH_SYNTHETIC_FIXTURE=1

# Injects the component workbench at /workbench (web/astro.config.mjs). Must be
# exactly "1". CI test/preview builds only — the production build never sets it,
# which is what keeps the production surface equal to the product pages.
#
# WORKBENCH=1

# Origin the Lighthouse transferred-bytes gate measures
# (web/scripts/check-lighthouse-budget.ts). Defaults to the local preview
# server at http://127.0.0.1:4321; override only to point the gate at an
# already-running server. Listed for completeness: it affects no build output.
#
# PREVIEW_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4321

# --- Why there is no default -------------------------------------------------
#
# `resolveLoadOptionsFromEnv` used to end in `return { dir: "test/fixtures/
# export" }`, and its doc comment claimed "the real production deploy pipeline
# always sets EXPORT_URL or EXPORT_DIR explicitly, never relies on this
# default". The Dockerfile disproved that sentence in one line: `RUN npm run
# build` with neither variable set, after `COPY web/ .` had brought the fixture
# into the build context. Every image built from this repository therefore
# published invented numbers as INE statistics, with no disclosure anywhere on
# the page — `2002-Q1: 11.85 % población activa, Definitivo` was read straight
# out of the real built HTML.
#
# The lesson is the same one the DATABASE_URL bug taught and this file's closing
# section records: a comment asserting how deployment behaves is not a
# mechanism. The mechanism is that the build now fails.


# =============================================================================
# OPERATIONS — optional
# =============================================================================

# Compose service name of the PostgreSQL container, used by
# scripts/backup/pg_dump.sh. Change only if the service is renamed.
# COMPOSE_POSTGRES_SERVICE=postgres

# Compose project name. Useful to run an isolated stack (a smoke test, a
# staging instance) beside the real one without colliding on volume or
# container names.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=concontexto


# =============================================================================
# NOT USED YET — reserved by the roadmap, listed so nobody re-invents them
# =============================================================================
#
# Nothing here is read by any code yet. Each arrives with its slice:
#   slice 5a  raw-file archive path, if it stops being the fixed /app_data mount
#   slice 9   alerting destination for failed ingestion / failed validation
#   Fase 3    public API key store and rate-limit configuration (PRD §11.4)
#
# Add them here in the SAME commit that first reads them, never later.


# =============================================================================
# Keeping this file honest
# =============================================================================
#
# The DATABASE_URL bug proved that deployment wiring is an integration
# surface with NO unit-test coverage: every Go test passed while the compose
# file was missing a variable the binary required, so `migrate up` would have
# silently done nothing on the VPS.
#
# The guard against a repeat is `scripts/check-env-example.sh`, run in CI: it
# fails the build when a name passed to os.Getenv / os.LookupEnv anywhere in
# the Go source is absent from this file. Documentation that CI enforces is
# documentation that stays true.
#
# THAT GUARD COVERS THE GO SOURCE ONLY, and the "WEB BUILD" block above is the
# proof that the gap matters: EXPORT_URL and EXPORT_DIR had existed since slice
# 9a, one of them decided which numbers the production image published, and
# neither appeared in this file, in docs/ or in openspec/config.yaml. No guard
# noticed, because no guard was looking at `process.env` in `web/`. Extending
# the script to the TypeScript sources is the obvious next step and is NOT done
# here; until it is, this block is maintained by hand and by review.
